   "reading": "It helped once (-6.1% at R=100), did nothing four times, and was 0.4% WORSE than the protocol once. Twice out of six the partition with no cheap half was the fastest, so counting cheap halves is not a reliable predictor when R is fixed. Net mean -1.0%, driven entirely by the single R=100 case.",
   "why": "When B is free you can move to a batch whose whole signature is cheap, and that was worth +15% at production. When R is fixed you can only choose the split, both halves rarely land cheap, and the remaining spread between partitions (2-4%) is the same size as the residual sawtooth INSIDE a signature, which the signature does not capture.",
